{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-132402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/bfa591e51f42e8e52b67e61c23b02ea69b03bdecc1c4b815370ce6b2b0b5f213.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $x={y}^{2}-4$ and $x=4-{y}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/85d3faac9a27353a8ea5151b3a32f1065aecb02fd07e0b20727c35de0a7fb216.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}-4\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}-4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132402" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/bb3249a9580e754c4ef9c33239c2a51a9b57ca9032a6acbeef7bbc532b2d4345.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}+2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/5df807dc424366c043211f7d3a1667bc2c9888f24292856ed99a07d7ac586a94.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=3-(y-3)^{2},\\,$ $x=(y-3)^{2}+4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/3191f16e4c72739688fa4243b44a374a027b307b6e4d419217545e924e1c7a4a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,3-(y-3)^{2}\\le x\\le (y-3)^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-(y-3)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3-(y-3)^{2}}[{\\int }_{3}^{(y-4)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{(y-3)^{2}+4}^{3-(y-3)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/df512140098a6fc6adc03e4d98199896cf15ed1cdd40578aea14ff1e83dfeba3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-40575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-y}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{-y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{4}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/5197029ed6803a39bf6d6d14bbdd8b3a71d8905e854bf3842305828280cbc39b.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,$ $x=6-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1761963e320e603b9c94ea4bab2df6f6ee8cc1b92cc0ca45bc84c8aeda1d3a11.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le x\\le 6-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{6-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{6-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{2}^{{y}^{2}/2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}/2}[{\\int }_{2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-40596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}xy\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{2-y}^{y/2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}xy\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/d90e750755a9520328e99583bfc80d9498ed34eb1695ba4ecd55b688d5b0216f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{2}$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/eeadcddee3bc966372613ded9304c723768e9b81cd0299ad14084a94c9348463.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/6d6597bff5d28155e9a40f953b0ab0652f175e4d4d0aaf6f28c50e6d5bc907d2.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=y-1,\\,$ $x=5-\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{y}{2}+5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}y & =6 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e2de1b62c6f3d0a15c0b95e3204f7889f6fc1ffbe108bdba7871c150091f6977.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,y-1\\le x\\le 5-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{5-y/2}^{y-1}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{5-y/2}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{4}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{y-1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ where $x>0,$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2},$ below the line $y=2.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/2209e57f8cbe84ccf2f930c5cd3004ff5009dfb72f3e4b3262517cf9b8f2b119.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y=\\frac{x}{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =2y \\\\ 2y-\\frac{1}{y} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{y}^{2}-1}{y} & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies in the first quadrant below the line $y=2$ the $y$-limits are \n$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1b03741537734087836c22dbb40e2e188825bade657cff129374efe1596bcb3c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{y}\\le x\\le 2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1/y}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1/y}{\\int }_{2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{2y}^{1/y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f390e83f737b3b20931df5e97a9ea0750b8b190acba14d1cd963aaab5175ba50.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2 & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)+{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+2{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =0 \\\\ (3y+2)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$,\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1b67c65c78dc847c2bf4bf53ad67f770a1406570548497edd55939c3a68a4c5c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-{y}^{2}}^{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}(y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2-\\pi "] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-37996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-37996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x-\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{{y}^{2}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-37996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-37996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-37996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\pi -\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-37996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "1-\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "e+1"] ["math" "e-1"] ["math" "e-\\frac{1}{e}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132421" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-\\frac{1}{e}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81320" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81320" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{27}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{y-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)-1)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)-1)\\,dy & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-1)-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-1)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1)-0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)"] ["math" "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)+1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/d5f54020da277980a58151bf2a85911365e67ecae6832881e03ac8b9b6431e6a.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}4x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} & =\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/d8b8b05e9004f6b25f635e9c39421a8e8e27831a26a22273cf88572df89ffc45.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}4x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2{[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2[4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,[3-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{1}{16}{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})dy & =6{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{48}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{64}{48}) \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132562" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${∬}_{D}y\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\,x\\,]_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y({y}^{2}+1-2{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/ff3b9f52ed01d991d1fff18e78c494488b53703669b6bb326b5436a19c952a18.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ as shown in the picture." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}} \\\\ 9-{y}^{2} & =3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/91f377562a1e94218b7be886cd63638e8881b0c4fac231a810bb6bff3a1fe643.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{y}^{2}-(3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[6y-\\frac{2}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(18-6-0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81395" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-81395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-81395" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/a135b0766239bcd6e9cacc324572486e66e1c859ecd28a4d59ef7795cc511ed4.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $x=0,$ above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/373286c03cc595535a7f04987e7454be2fac547d769341343f05ea67ee0b001d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{[\\,\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y\\,]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2})ydy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4y-{y}^{3})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4y-{y}^{3})dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-40680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-40680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}-0)dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "991e8cfa37ad26077354752d2d5afadfdf9755c26a7d71df2a82f7e590fe0e47"} {"question" "q-132531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "q-132531" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/state.json" "file_sha256" "118bf0edde96d9831cc7f51eefd7e9e0e8064bf692edee4cced1af76ca1e675e"} {"question" "e-7134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7134.json" "file_sha256" "3dbe3420cb9a63b391b409c725eafc388dd0081c57a5e0bcefc3cacfab7bcf3a"} {"question" "e-7134" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=1-x & \\,⟹\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f0e531c612c84aa0260b318aa1fbe21c9089270b8c4a1079e08583b7fc5a5d90.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x  \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{{y}^{2}+2}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7134.json" "file_sha256" "3dbe3420cb9a63b391b409c725eafc388dd0081c57a5e0bcefc3cacfab7bcf3a"} {"question" "e-7135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7135.json" "file_sha256" "0f43db4965c26c2d5f7d7f58a0311fb4e57bc165a3d583a5745a2bd25ff703ab"} {"question" "e-7135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/ffbe0db1ae1bb082e63b0d97324b43dd78334dfbd1e88aaa0744be039dd0efc6.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=3-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3-y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3-y}{2} \\\\ 4y & =(3-y)^{2} \\\\ 4y & =9-6y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies below the line $y=3,$ the $y$-limits are \n\n$$\n1 \\le y \\le 3.\n$$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/3c56798a33ee2da6d928174b67546945be4bf3594d15e89f3ca39119dd4e2c62.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{(3-y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7135.json" "file_sha256" "0f43db4965c26c2d5f7d7f58a0311fb4e57bc165a3d583a5745a2bd25ff703ab"} {"question" "e-7136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7136.json" "file_sha256" "adce5dba90740ba5f31c1e33e6f82e8710749259414a02c3a3ad20fe22f6094b"} {"question" "e-7136" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}]}_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7136.json" "file_sha256" "adce5dba90740ba5f31c1e33e6f82e8710749259414a02c3a3ad20fe22f6094b"} {"question" "e-7137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7137.json" "file_sha256" "2b2831b3cbe10faa472d18982d46224cd7be35050e57d66b5319ad6bbc1943ac"} {"question" "e-7137" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x \\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$-1 \\le y \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/8d77e3caa18191710e056b72ea54e5820e6244d9e90719222deed409d3992c5b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\,   \\mathrm{d}  y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{10}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{32}{10})-(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/example-7137.json" "file_sha256" "2b2831b3cbe10faa472d18982d46224cd7be35050e57d66b5319ad6bbc1943ac"}] "usage" {"e-7134" [] "e-7135" [] "e-7136" [] "e-7137" [] "q-40596" [] "q-40575" []} "basis" 1817 "retractions" [[17592186134222 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y = 2x$ and $y =-x$ from $y = 0$ to $y = 4$. What is $∬_(D)(x^{2} + y^{2})dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186134223 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/q-40596.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x = 2 - y$, $x = \\frac{y}{2}$, and $y = 0$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)xydA =$"] [17592186133576 :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1$, as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/77c8f86821d718dbfe450f51148a59e3.png)"] [17592186133576 :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nand $y=1-x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\sqrt{x - 2} & ⟹x = y^{2} + 2 \\\\ y = 1 - x & ⟹x = 1 - y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/6bcc7bf82e91ec4c15377d04f25681af.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x^{2}y + xy^{2})dA &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{1 - y}^{y^{2} + 2}(x^{2}y + xy^{2})dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133577 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3$. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left({x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?"] [17592186133577 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/1c78772e8e145e3e09dbc2d763e7f1cb.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = x^{2} & ⟹x = \\sqrt{y} \\\\ y = 3 - 2x & ⟹x = \\frac{3 - y}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{y}|= \\frac{3 - y}{2}; 4y|= (3 - y)^{2}; 4y|= 9 - 6y + y^{2}; y^{2} - 10y + 9|= 0; (y - 1)(y - 9)|= 0$So, since our region lies below the line $y=3$, the $y$-limits are\n$1 \\le y \\le 3$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/d3c6291cf3fb20c734e14cf5a74027de.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∫_{1}^{3}∫_{(3 - y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133578 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y$."] [17592186133578 :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}∫_{0}^{y^{2}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}dxdy &= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}[∫_{0}^{y^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}(y^{2} - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}y\\sin y^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}y\\sin y^{2}dy &= [- \\frac{1}{2}\\cos y^{2}]_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos 2π - \\cos π) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133579 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0$, and above the line $y=-1$, as shown in the picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/d3efc009081220a488deb11b6d536fce.png)"] [17592186133579 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following equations in terms of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y^{2} = 4 & ⟹x = \\sqrt{4 - y^{2}} \\\\ x = 0 & ⟹x = 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}|= 0; 4 - y^{2}|= 0; y^{2}|= 4; y|= ± 2$.\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1$, the $y$-limits are\n$-1 \\le y \\le 2$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/8b3b3ffed1e4aeb6dd4c9e503c34687d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\, \\mathrm{d} y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}xy^{2}dxdy &= ∫_{-1}^{2}y^{2}[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}xdx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}y^{2}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}\\frac{y^{2}}{2}(4 - y^{2} - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}(2y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{2}(2y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2})dy &= [\\frac{2}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{y^{5}}{10}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{16}{3} - \\frac{32}{10}) - (-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{10}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131775 17592186131776 17592186131777 17592186131778 17592186131779 17592186131781 17592186131782 17592186131783 17592186131784 17592186131785] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131774 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131774 :answer-field/id] [17592186131774 :answer-field/key] [17592186131774 :answer-field/type] [17592186131780 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131780 :answer-field/id] [17592186131780 :answer-field/key] [17592186131780 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
